{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786509746,"nonce":"QLJ8KhyE6wuexflB0xdw9f6JHyc=","title":"**What is wrong.** Nothing is wrong with the schema. What is missing is a written answer to a question that keeps getting asked: should the high-growth tables be RANGE-partitioned the way the event tables are? The question has now been investigated end to end, and the answer is no — not as these tables stand, and for most of them not ever. That answer is currently nowhere in the repo, so it will be asked again.","message":"Two facts frame it. First, the event tables anyone would reason from are **already** partitioned: `alert_events` (monthly), `ingest_batches` and `ingest_rejections` (daily), `ui_events` (hourly), all managed by `oto_partitions_manage`. Second, Postgres requires a unique index on a partitioned table to include the partition key. The SPEC already hit this and already solved it once. SPEC §C14: *\"Architect proposes unique indexes for idempotency on partitioned tables (`alert_events`, `ingest_batches`). Postgres requires a unique index on a partitioned table to include the partition key — so these do not actually dedupe.\"* → *\"Idempotency moves to two small **unpartitioned** side tables: `alert_event_keys` and `ingest_dedup`.\"* That row is not history. `alert_event_keys` exists (`00007_alerts.sql:288`) and is the template for any future work here.\n\n**The demonstration.** Eight tables remain unpartitioned and unreaped. In each case the constraint that a partition key would have to join is the product invariant itself, so adding a date does not weaken performance — it silently converts a GLOBAL guarantee into a PER-PARTITION one, and the DDL keeps compiling.\n\n- `alerts` — `alerts_key_uniq UNIQUE (org_id, alert_key)` (`00007_alerts.sql:54`) — dedup identity. `00007_alerts.sql:101`: *\"Dedup is enforced by alerts_key_uniq, NEVER by a read-then-write check.\"* An alert re-seen after its partition rolls becomes a second Alert with `first_seen_at` reset.\n- `alert_occurrences` — `occ_one_open_idx UNIQUE (alert_id) WHERE ended_at IS NULL` (`00007_alerts.sql:187`) — one open episode per alert. The comment above it: *\"at most one open occurrence per alert. Enforced in the DB, not in Go. An Occurrence is a CONTIGUOUS episode; two open at once is definitionally a bug.\"* A date in that index permits exactly two open episodes either side of a boundary. `occ_seq_uniq UNIQUE (alert_id, seq)` breaks the same way: two episodes both claiming seq=4.\n- `notifications` — `notifications_idem_uniq UNIQUE (org_id, idempotency_key)` (`00011_notification.sql:219`) — the §C.7 intent identity. An at-least-once `notify.evaluate` retried across a month boundary inserts a second intent and fans out a second Slack post.\n- `notification_deliveries` — `deliveries_fanout_uniq UNIQUE (notification_id, channel_id, mode)` (`00024_delivery_mode_uniq.sql:26`) — fan-out idempotency. A date permits a duplicate Slack message for the same fact on the same channel.\n- `channel_threads` — `threads_subject_uniq UNIQUE (channel_id, subject_kind, subject_id)` (`00011_notification.sql:168`) — one generation owns exactly one thread (ADR 0005). A date lets one generation own two.\n- `rule_snapshots` — `rule_snapshots_content_uniq UNIQUE (org_id, source_id, rule_name, rule_group, rule_file, rule_fingerprint)` (`00040:58`) — content addressing. A date destroys it and manufactures false §C.6 drift at every boundary, because drift is defined as the newest snapshot differing from the one bound to the previous occurrence.\n- `alert_groups` — `groups_key_gen_uniq UNIQUE (org_id, group_key, generation)`, with three inbound FKs (`alert_group_members.group_id`, `alert_occurrences.group_id`, `notifications.group_id`) — the widest blast radius of the set.\n- `alert_group_members` — the PK would gain `joined_at`, weakening \"one membership row per (group, occurrence)\", and buys nothing.\n\nEven where the invariant could be relocated, pruning would buy close to nothing, because none of these tables' hot queries carry a time bound. The §D.12(a) alert-list keyset orders on `(last_seen_at DESC, id DESC)` with no lower bound, so partitioning `alerts` on `first_seen_at` prunes zero partitions and turns `alerts_list_idx` into an all-partition merge append. `notif_subject_idx` and `notif_alert_idx` are subject point-lookups. `del_thread_seq_idx` and `del_retry_idx` are global partial indexes a background sweep scans deliberately. One index in the whole set is time-ordered: `del_dead_idx (org_id, created_at DESC)`.\n\n**Why it matters.** The one genuinely right instinct in the question is about `notification_deliveries`, and it should not be lost with the rest. It has the highest growth rate of the eight and the only time-ordered hot index, so it is the real future candidate — first in line if anything here is ever partitioned.\n\nIts growth model is worth stating plainly, because the intuitive one is wrong. `notification_deliveries` grows per fan-out target — per (notification × channel × mode) — and **never per delivery attempt**. `attempts` is a counter incremented in place by `claimSQL` (`attempts = attempts + 1`, `deliveries.go:288`, capped at 32 by `deliveries_attempts_ck`); failure, retry and requeue all UPDATE that same row. Since `00024` each mode carries its own row, so `update_root` plus `thread_reply` for one fact is 2 rows, not 1 and not 32. A table retried hard for a week does not grow at all.\n\nThe schema change it would need is exactly the §C14 pattern: `deliveries_fanout_uniq` moves to a small unpartitioned `delivery_keys` side table, and the FK to `notifications` needs the denormalised partition key if `notifications` moves too. `notifications` can only follow, never lead — its FK child has to move in the same change.\n\nThere is also a cost the request did not price. `oto_partitions_manage` has a **hardcoded parent list**: the helpers (`oto_partition_name`, `oto_ensure_partition`, `oto_ensure_partitions_ahead`, `oto_drop_partitions_before`) are generic and take `(parent, grain, …)`, but the orchestrator is four literal `IF to_regclass('public.\u003cname\u003e') IS NOT NULL THEN … RETURN NEXT; END IF;` blocks with parent, grain, ahead-count and retention parameter written in (`00005_partitions.sql:174-211`, re-emitted by `00036_retention_defaults.sql:40-89`). A fifth parent costs: a migration re-emitting the whole `CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION` body; a decision on which retention window drives it, where a new parameter changes the signature and in Postgres creates an overload rather than a replacement, so the old 3-arg function must be DROPped; `internal/app/workers.go:249` plus `managePartitions`/`effectiveRetention`; `test/integration/am_route_timings_test.go:433`, which selects on `pronargs = 3` and substring-matches the rendered argument list, and so fails on any signature change; and `test/harness/postgres.go:265`, which calls `SELECT oto_partitions_manage()` with zero arguments relying on the DEFAULTs, so a new parameter needs one.\n\nNone of this contradicts the documented position; it confirms it. ADR 0024 §1: *\"Four tables age out, and only four. Everything else in the schema is unpartitioned and has no reaper.\"* … *\"**Never reaped, by anything, at any setting:** `alerts`, `alert_occurrences`, `alert_groups`, `alert_group_members`, `rule_snapshots`, `notifications`, `notification_deliveries`, `channel_threads`, `enrichments`, `silences`, `alert_quality_daily`, `alert_event_keys`.\"* Conclusion: *\"**retention deletes the narrative, never the record.**\"* And ADR 0014's own answer to scale on these tables is rollups (`alert_quality_daily`), not more partitions.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00005_partitions.sql:174-211` and `00036_retention_defaults.sql:40-89` (the hardcoded orchestrator), the eight constraints listed above, `docs/design/SPEC.md:64` (§C14), `docs/adr/0014-postgres-only-no-analytical-store.md:40-44`, `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:25-41,77`.\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** No migration is written. The finding is captured where the decision lives — an amendment to ADR 0024 is the natural home, since it already owns the never-reaped list — recording that partitioning these eight was evaluated and declined, the per-table constraint that blocks each one, and that §C14's side-table pattern is the only route through. The ADR 0014 revisit triggers are the reopen condition, monitored and not merely written down: **alert-list p95 above 200 ms with a warm cache; a rollup job overrunning its own interval; a single org exceeding roughly 10M events per month.** And `notification_deliveries` has a written design ready for the day one of those fires — the `delivery_keys` side table, the denormalised partition key on the FK from `notifications`, the grain, and the fifth-parent cost above — so that day is an execution, not another investigation.","files":null}]}